.Dd June 9, 2020
.Dt REBOOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm reboot ,
.Nm halt ,
.Nm fastboot ,
.Nm fasthalt
.Nd stopping and restarting the system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm halt
.Op Fl Dflnpq
.Op Fl e Ar variable=value
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Nm
.Op Fl Ddflnpq
.Op Fl e Ar variable=value
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Nm fasthalt
.Op Fl Dflnpq
.Op Fl e Ar variable=value
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Nm fastboot
.Op Fl Ddflnpq
.Op Fl e Ar variable=value
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm halt
and
.Nm
utilities flush the file system cache to disk, send all running processes
a
.Dv SIGTERM
(and subsequently a
.Dv SIGKILL )
and, respectively, halt or restart the system.
The action is logged, including entering a shutdown record into the
.Xr wtmpx 5
file.
.Pp
The
.Fl D ,
.Fl e ,
.Fl k
and
.Fl o
options write
.Pa /boot/nextboot.conf ,
which the loader applies to the next boot only.
This makes it possible to try an alternate kernel and fall back to the
previous one if it does not boot.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl D
Delete an existing
.Nm nextboot
configuration and exit.
.It Fl d
The system is requested to create a crash dump.
This option is
supported only when rebooting, and it has no effect unless a dump
device has previously been specified with
.Xr dumpon 8 .
.It Fl e Ar variable=value
Set
.Va variable
to
.Va value
for the next boot.
The assignment is applied like a
.Pa loader.conf
line: tunables (names containing a dot) and variables that the loader
exports are placed in the kernel environment, other names stay loader
variables.
The value is quoted when it is written to the
.Nm nextboot
configuration.
.It Fl f
Forced mode.
Normally the alternate kernel is checked for before the configuration
is written,
.Pa /boot
must be a UFS file system, and a failure writing the configuration
aborts the operation.
With this flag, failed checks and write failures only produce
warnings.
.It Fl k Ar kernel
Boot the specified kernel
.Ar kernel
on the next system boot.
This is a one-shot option; the default kernel is booted on successive
boots.
No
.Nm
or
.Nm halt
is performed if
.Pa /boot/ Ns Ar kernel Ns Pa /kernel
does not exist unless the
.Fl f
flag is specified.
.It Fl l
The halt or reboot is
.Em not
logged to the system log.
This option is intended for applications such as
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
that call
.Nm
or
.Nm halt
and log this themselves.
.It Fl n
The file system cache is not flushed.
This option should probably not be used.
.It Fl o Ar options
Pass
.Ar options
to the kernel on the next boot.
.It Fl p
The system will turn off the power if it can.
If the power down action fails, the system
will halt or reboot normally, depending on whether
.Nm halt
or
.Nm
was called.
.It Fl q
The system is halted or restarted quickly and ungracefully, and only
the flushing of the file system cache is performed (if the
.Fl n
is not specified).
This option should probably not be used.
.El
.Pp
The
.Nm fasthalt
and
.Nm fastboot
utilities are nothing more than aliases for the
.Nm halt
and
.Nm
utilities.
.Pp
Normally, the
.Xr shutdown 8
utility is used when the system needs to be halted or restarted, giving
users advance warning of their impending doom and cleanly terminating
specific programs.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr wtmpx 5 ,
.Xr boot 8 ,
.Xr dumpon 8 ,
.Xr savecore 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
.Xr sync 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.Bx 4.0 .
